cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  human-ai-taskcore (ptx): a closed-form physics-to-perception transfer model
  for task-based medical imaging
message: >-
  If you use this software, please cite it. A result should cite the version DOI
  of the release it was produced with, not the concept DOI below.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Yamamoto
    given-names: Shuji
    email: yamamoto@lisit.jp
    orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9211-1071'
    affiliation: Institute of One, LISIT Co., Ltd., Tokyo, Japan
repository-code: https://github.com/Institute-of-One/human_ai_taskcore
url: https://github.com/Institute-of-One/human_ai_taskcore
abstract: >-
  A Python package that carries a detection task from a CT reconstruction
  through display transfer and luminance mapping, ocular optics, and the neural
  internal noise of a published contrast-sensitivity model, and integrates it
  into a task-weighted detectability. From the frequency-resolved integrand it
  derives a perceptual saturation frequency f_sat, a perceptual utilisation
  ratio R_perceptual, a gain per unit dose G_useful, and a sufficient
  magnification M*. Visual sensitivity enters through the effective noise as
  Barten's own internal noise rather than as a weight on the signal: with a
  numerator weight and no noise floors, any invertible linear filter cancels
  exactly out of the detectability integral, so reconstruction kernel and
  display transfer would be undescribable in principle. That cancellation is
  retained as a validation result. The external validation against published
  human observer studies was run once, over a pool closed before any correlation
  was computed, under criteria frozen before the literature search; it covers CT
  only, which is the consequence the pre-registration fixed in advance when the
  non-CT pool slot could not be filled. No imaging data of any kind, and none of
  the published papers screened, are redistributed.
keywords:
  - task-based image quality
  - model observer
  - contrast sensitivity
  - detectability
  - saturation frequency
  - ultra-high-resolution CT
  - display conditions
  - pre-registration
  - reproducibility
license: MIT
version: 0.1.0
# The concept DOI goes here once Zenodo has minted one, and it is deliberately absent
# rather than guessed: it does not exist until the first deposit. The concept DOI
# resolves to whichever release is latest, which is what a citation of the *project*
# should point at; a citation of a *result* must name the version DOI of the release
# that produced it, which is why results/release.json holds that one separately and the
# manuscript quotes it from there.
references:
  - type: book
    title: >-
      Contrast Sensitivity of the Human Eye and Its Effects on Image Quality
    doi: 10.1117/3.353254
    year: 1999
    notes: >-
      The visual model this software implements: pupil diameter, ocular MTF, the
      contrast sensitivity function, and the photon-plus-neural internal noise
      density the effective noise term is built from.
    authors:
      - family-names: Barten
        given-names: Peter G. J.
  - type: standard
    title: 'DICOM PS3.14: Grayscale Standard Display Function'
    year: 2011
    notes: >-
      The display luminance mapping, implemented from the rational polynomial of
      equation 7-1 and checked against its tabulated values.
    authors:
      - name: National Electrical Manufacturers Association
  - type: article
    title: >-
      Human- and model-observer performance in ramp-spectrum noise: effects of
      regularization and object variability
    doi: 10.1364/JOSAA.18.000473
    year: 2001
    notes: The dense difference-of-Gaussian channel form used by the channelised observer.
    authors:
      - family-names: Abbey
        given-names: Craig K.
      - family-names: Barrett
        given-names: Harrison H.
  - type: article
    title: >-
      Statistically defined backgrounds: performance of a modified
      nonprewhitening observer model
    doi: 10.1364/JOSAA.11.001237
    year: 1994
    notes: The non-prewhitening observer with an eye filter.
    authors:
      - family-names: Burgess
        given-names: Arthur E.
  - type: article
    title: Addition of a channel mechanism to the ideal-observer model
    doi: 10.1364/JOSAA.4.002447
    year: 1987
    notes: The channelised Hotelling observer.
    authors:
      - family-names: Myers
        given-names: Kyle J.
      - family-names: Barrett
        given-names: Harrison H.
